WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.130
I’m marking at a café, on the free Wi-Fi.

2
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.840
I’ve just signed in to the school portal.

3
00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.690
Should I worry about who

4
00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.130
else is on this network?

5
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.420
Today: what others can see on Wi-Fi,

6
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.580
and how to set up yours safely.

7
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.240
Whoever runs a Wi-Fi network can see

8
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.760
which websites your phone visits.

9
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.540
With a plain HTTP page, which your

10
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.060
browser marks “Not secure”, they can

11
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.500
read everything you send.

12
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:34.510
With HTTPS, the padlock, what’s inside is

13
00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.930
scrambled: they see the website’s name,

14
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.410
but not your password or your marks.

15
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.780
And here’s the catch.

16
00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.910
Anyone can set up a hotspot and call

17
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.750
it “Free Bus Station WiFi”.

18
00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.830
A fake hotspot sees everything a

19
00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.170
real one sees, and it may show

20
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.370
you a fake sign-in page.

21
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.400
A name on a list proves nothing.

22
00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.010
So here’s the rule.

23
00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.560
On public Wi-Fi, read the news or

24
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.260
watch a video: that’s fine.

25
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.490
But for mobile money, banking, email and

26
00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.030
pupils’ marks, switch to mobile data.

27
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.420
And turn off auto-join, so your phone

28
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.320
doesn’t connect by itself to any

29
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.800
network with a familiar name.

30
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:19.730
Quick check.

31
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.720
I need to pay a bill with mobile

32
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.380
money at the bus station.

33
00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.320
I can see “Bus Station Free” and

34
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.560
“Bus Station Free WiFi”.

35
00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.370
Which one should I use?

36
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:34.210
Neither.

37
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.710
For money, switch to mobile data, which

38
00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.270
goes straight to your phone company.

39
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.710
A few megabytes of data cost far less

40
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.690
than an emptied mobile money account.

41
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:46.900
What about a VPN?

42
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.340
A VPN wraps your traffic in a tunnel, so

43
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.780
the café owner sees nothing but the

44
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:54.100
tunnel.

45
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.340
But the VPN company can

46
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.620
see it all instead.

47
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.900
So avoid unknown free VPN apps: if it’s

48
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.320
free, your data may be what it sells.

49
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.880
Now your own Wi-Fi, at home or at school.

50
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.580
Change the router’s admin password,

51
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.760
because the one it came with is

52
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.600
often easy to find or guess.

53
00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.940
Choose WPA2 or WPA3 security,

54
00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.220
with a long Wi-Fi password.

55
00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.190
And update the router,

56
00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.350
just like your phone.

57
00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.430
At school, give visitors and

58
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:30.790
pupils a guest network.

59
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.460
Keep the office computers, with the

60
00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.320
records, on a separate network.

61
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.630
And don’t write the Wi-Fi

62
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.390
password on the classroom wall.

63
00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:41.690
If you share your phone’s hotspot,

64
00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.070
give it a password, and switch it

65
00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:44.710
off when you’re done.

66
00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:46.690
Your turn.

67
00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.550
Our new school router still uses the

68
00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:51.030
admin password it came with.

69
00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.030
Is that safe to leave?

70
00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.560
No: change it, because the

71
00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.520
passwords routers come with are

72
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.560
often easy to find or guess.

73
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:05.450
Remember: HTTPS hides what’s inside, a

74
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.490
network’s name proves nothing, money goes

75
00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.350
over mobile data, and your router gets a

76
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:16.150
new password, WPA2 or WPA3, and updates.

77
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.760
Try it in class tomorrow: draw a café, a

78
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.740
phone and a website on the board.

79
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.730
Pupils draw what travels between them,

80
00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.530
and mark what the café owner can see,

81
00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.010
with and without the padlock.
